      {
        "figure": "figure2_cross_cohort_panel_effects",
        "labels": [],
        "caption": "Figure 2. Unadjusted panel effects across 17 primary design contrasts. Numbers show mean log2 effects; an asterisk denotes BH q < 0.05 across all 232 panel tests, including metadata sensitivities. Positive effects follow the numerator in each row. Cb, cerebellum; Hip, hippocampus; HGC, habitat ground control; VGC, vivarium control. OSD-563, OSD-564 and OSD-612 are three regions of one RR-10 animal cohort, not independent missions. Glial and endothelial transcript abundance does not directly measure inflammation or barrier permeability. Six displayed panels are selected from the eight tested; all tests remain in the supplied tables."
      },
      {
        "figure": "figure3_count_model_and_biology",
        "labels": [
          "a",
          "b"
        ],
        "caption": "Figure 3. Count-model robustness and biological candidate sensitivity. (a) The 120 panel comparisons summarize 15 regional contrasts fitted using PyDESeq2; agreement is analytical robustness on the same observations, not independent replication. (b) Full and metadata-excluded hippocampal ISS-T flight contrasts for SRG and circadian panels. Circles are log-expression effects with 1,000-animal-bootstrap percentile intervals; hollow diamonds show mean unshrunk negative-binomial effects for the full data. q values use the complete 232-test panel family. Exclusions remove the older FLT ISS-T support; the full SRG result does not establish a region-specific mechanism."
      },
      {
        "figure": "figure4_paired_regional_interactions",
        "labels": [
          "a",
          "b",
          "c",
          "d"
        ],
        "caption": "Figure 4. Paired regional interactions. (a) Source Name matching identifies 24 RRRM-2 and 10 RR-10 cerebellum\u2013hippocampus animal pairs. (b) All eight panel effects in primary regional contrasts; no test has q\u22640.05 across the complete 104-test family, including sensitivities. (c) Individual SRG hippocampus-minus-cerebellum scores; outlined points identify metadata conflicts. (d) SRG regional flight coefficients with 2,000-animal-bootstrap percentile intervals and complete-family q values. The approximate three-way interaction uses 9,999 restricted-model wild draws. Metadata exclusion removes all older FLT ISS-T animals, reducing the sensitivity to younger-animal exposure support."
      },
      {
        "figure": "figure5_bounded_operator_and_joint_uncertainty",
        "labels": [
          "a",
          "b",
          "c"
        ],
        "caption": "Figure 5. Bounded score geometry and joint uncertainty. (a) Unbounded score gain for all 15 aligned anchor\u2013target geometries; the bounded operator has gain at most two. (b) Observed sensor-panel effect-energy removal for matched hippocampal recovery and the RR-23 shared-HGC comparison. (c) Observed outside-sensor energy removal and 500-bootstrap percentile intervals, refitting normalization, anchor and target on each resample. Shared RR-23 HGC animals are sampled once for both roles. Intervals remain conditional on the fixed initially retained gene universe and observational sampling assumptions; zero crossings and negative reductions are shown. A point estimate may lie outside a biased percentile interval. Energy removal does not measure biological recovery."
      },
      {
        "figure": "figure6_ground_holdout",
        "labels": [
          "a",
          "b",
          "c",
          "d"
        ],
        "caption": "Figure 6. Ground-only prediction and correction holdouts. (a) Within-age sensor and whole-genome AUC; (b) outside-sensor expression prediction error relative to a training-fitted intercept/age baseline; (c) training loading gain; (d) held-out outside-sensor collection-contrast energy removal. Points are split medians and lines are 2.5th\u201397.5th split quantiles, not independent-sample confidence intervals. Core regions use all 81 age-balanced holdouts each; RR-23 uses 200 prespecified distinct splits. Filtering, normalization references, nuisance means and scores use training data only. Five nested-CV strengths are selected only within training animals. The 362 splits overlap and involve 30 unique ground source animals. Projection is excluded from disjoint-sensor prediction because it uses response genes in its score. Reliable sensor discrimination does not establish biological recovery."
      },
      {
        "figure": "figure7_ground_anchor_transfer",
        "labels": [],
        "caption": "Figure 7. Ground-anchor sensitivity and transfer. Dots show the observed change in squared contrast coefficients outside the Sensor31 genes after genome-expression projection or ground-calibrated score subtraction. Lines show 95% percentile ranges from 200 ground-animal bootstraps within age and cohort, conditional on normalized counts and a fixed target. These ranges describe anchor uncertainty, not complete sampling intervals or calibrated gene-level inference. The within-OSD-525 evaluation shares nine HGC animals with its anchor and is displayed as such. Negative reductions are retained. Removing expression variation is not proof of removing confounding."
      },
      {
        "figure": "figure8_disjoint_anchor_partitions",
        "labels": [
          "a",
          "b"
        ],
        "caption": "Figure 8. RR-23 disjoint-anchor partitions. Each of 126 partitions trains on four of nine HGC animals plus the same eight VGC animals, then evaluates the nine FLT animals against the remaining five HGC animals. Training and evaluation animals are disjoint within a partition. Partitions reuse the same cohort and do not constitute 126 independent validations. (a) Removing sensor effect energy does not ensure a reduction outside the sensor. (b) \u03bb is derived from conditional OLS t probabilities and is a dispersion diagnostic, not a measure of biological truth or empirical false-positive rate."
      },
      {
        "figure": "figure9_known_truth_validation",
        "labels": [
          "a",
          "b",
          "c",
          "d"
        ],
        "caption": "Figure 9. Independent training\u2013target Gaussian mechanism benchmark. Five scenarios each contain 200 independently generated datasets (1,000 datasets; 7,000 method rows including additional oracle-ground and partial-correction references in the supplement). Four operational methods are shown without target tuning. Bars are \u00b11.96 Monte Carlo standard errors of the mean, not real-data confidence intervals. Biological FDP compares discoveries with the original biological coefficient vector and therefore differs from conditional statistical FDR under residual nuisance. Power and biological energy retention are undefined when no biological effects exist. Failure under overlapping biology or changed coupling is retained. The simulator is not a complete RNA-seq generative model. Panels show (a) effect RMSE, (b) biological FDP, (c) power and (d) known biological-vector energy retention."
      },
      {
        "figure": "figure10_high_gain_domain_shift",
        "labels": [
          "a",
          "b",
          "c",
          "d"
        ],
        "caption": "Figure 10. Exploratory high-gain stress benchmark. All 16 combinations of true gain (8 or 12), training size (24 or 96), target size (24 or 48) and unchanged or flipped outside-sensor nuisance coupling are shown. Each uses 100 independent repetitions (1,600 datasets; 6,400 method rows). Known biology is supported outside Sensor31 and is preserved exactly by both score operators. Error bars are \u00b11.96 Monte Carlo standard errors. Bounding reduces worst score amplification under coupling reversal but leaves substantial nuisance, including under unchanged coupling; neither norm control nor exact sensor-null preservation establishes accurate biological recovery. Panels (a,c) show unchanged-coupling RMSE and biological FDP; (b,d) show their flipped-coupling counterparts."
      },
      {
        "figure": "figureS1_cross_cohort_gene_screen",
        "labels": [
          "a",
          "b"
        ],
        "caption": "Supplementary Figure 1. Conservative regional gene reproducibility screen. A candidate must have the same effect direction in every constituent contrast and finite component Wald p values; its intersection\u2013union p value is the maximum component p. Discordant or unavailable effects receive p=1. All 54,989 region\u2013gene combinations enter one global BH and BY family. No gene survives either correction. Component comparisons come from the same negative-binomial fits and can share samples; concordant direction alone is not replication evidence. Ranked nominal candidates are explicitly exploratory."
      },
      {
        "figure": "figureS2_all_deposit_structure",
        "labels": [
          "a",
          "b",
          "c",
          "d",
          "e",
          "f",
          "g",
          "h",
          "i"
        ],
        "caption": "Supplementary Figure 2. Count-derived QC and expression structure across all seven deposits. (a\u2013g) PCA of the top 2,000 variable log-expression genes in each deposit; axes give variance fractions. Groups use deposited exposure/cohort design labels; older core animals are squares and location conflicts are outlined. (h) Descriptive multivariate condition partial R\u00b2 after age terms where available. (i) Mitochondrial gene-count fractions for every collapsed animal\u2013region observation. These fractions use gene-assigned counts and are not measures of RNA integrity, mitochondrial injury or causal exposure effects. PCA and R\u00b2 are descriptive; no discovery family is inferred from them."
      },
      {
        "figure": "figureS3_flight_collection_interaction",
        "labels": [
          "a",
          "b",
          "c",
          "d"
        ],
        "caption": "Supplementary Figure 3. Direct within-region flight by collection interactions. (a,b) Panel interaction estimates and stratified animal-bootstrap intervals for each region; the asterisk marks the one panel passing the complete 32-test BH family. (c) Gene coefficient agreement between normalized-log and count models. (d) Whole-animal age support in each design cell. The highlighted full-data interaction has wild p=0.000838 and q=0.026816; metadata-excluded q=0.786. The 499,999 restricted wild draws are approximate inference, and omitted effects are descriptive stability checks. Exclusions leave one older FLT ISS-T animal in hippocampus and none in cerebellum. A common additive age effect does not resolve interactions in an empty design cell."
      },
      {
        "figure": "figureS4_fixed_blood_marker_context",
        "labels": [
          "a",
          "b",
          "c",
          "d"
        ],
        "caption": "Supplementary Figure 4. Fixed erythroid-associated marker context. (a,b) Fixed marker-score distributions for every primary animal in each region; lines show group medians. (c) All seven count-model marker interaction coefficients with Wald intervals; asterisks refer to the existing complete gene family. (d) Marker-score versus Circadian-panel residual association after full-design adjustment. Target-omitted correlations for individual marker transcripts are supplied in the tables. No new marker significance family was selected. Beta-globin interactions motivate a possible composition contribution but do not establish blood contamination, a cell source, or an erythroid fraction. The complete tables retain markers failing the global family and all metadata sensitivities."
